WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.940
Edison paid half a million dollars to light one square mile with six custom dynamos. This

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:10.640
is First Principles Daily, episode 145. Pearl Street's price against the metal inside

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:15.880
the machines. The belt and engine habit those machines refused. The coupling and the matched

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:20.280
frames that closed part of the gap. And the copper under the street that the dynamo

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:26.420
redesign never touched. Thomas Edison opened the Pearl Street Station on September 4, 1882.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:33.460
627 ton jumbo dynamos fed direct current through underground conduits to roughly 1200 lamps in a

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:38.980
quarter square mile district of Lower Manhattan. Construction, including real estate, the building,

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:45.740
and the wiring, ran to about $300,000. All charges together pushed the bill past that construction

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:51.200
figure into the half million already on the table. That price was a machine built at the outer

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:56.020
edge of what existing shops could produce. It was not the lower limit set by the iron,

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:01.460
copper, and steel inside the machines. Wave a magic wand and arrange those atoms into a

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:06.980
finished dynamo for free, and you would pay only for the commodities. Cast iron for the frame,

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:12.740
copper for the windings, steel for the shaft. That commodity pile is the magic wand number.

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:18.880
The theoretical floor. The idiot index is how many times the finished thing cost above that floor.

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:24.440
A rough accounting for a 27 ton machine already showed the finished price sitting many times

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:26.240
above the value of the metal alone.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:32.200
Exact commodity prices for those castings are not a clean public ledger, so the index stays

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:35.140
a ratio you can see, not a measured dollar fact.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:40.220
The gap between metal and finished machine is exactly where Edison stopped copying and

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:41.260
started redesigning.

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:46.560
Before Pearl Street, electric lighting relied on small dynamos that drove arc lamps in

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:47.520
streets or factories.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:52.400
industries. Those machines were sized for one building or one block. Power reached the

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:56.900
load through belts or ropes from a separate steam engine. The convention assumed larger

00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:02.720
output simply meant scaling the same layout. Bigger frame, longer belts, more copper windings

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:07.900
wound by hand. Each installation stayed a one-off because no standard existed for central

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:13.360
generation at city scale. Builders copied parts and connections already proven in laboratory

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:19.380
or exhibition settings. High material use, high assembly labor, and every new plant repeating

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:25.100
the same pattern. Separate engine, transmission, and generator. Edison refused that incremental

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:30.060
path. The only way he saw to compete with gas lighting was a single station serving hundreds

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:35.320
of customers at once. The jumbo units therefore had to deliver ten times the output of any

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:40.400
prior dynamo, and they had to fit inside one building on Pearl Street. That requirement

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:43.960
exposed the cost penalty built into the old separate engine layout.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:48.860
Shops that had produced exhibition machines saw no reason to question the belt drive

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:51.160
because it had worked for smaller loads.

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:56.600
Scaling the same drive meant thicker belts, larger pulleys, and constant tension adjustments.

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:00.720
Those additions bought both extra parts and extra maintenance hours.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:05.680
Handwound field coils on each new unit repeated the same slow winding process

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:08.200
with no shared tooling between machines.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:13.320
The assumption that every generator needed its own dedicated engine and its own transmission

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:17.780
locked in repeated labor and repeated material waste across every installation.

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:23.440
Once you see the cost stack as interfaces rather than iron, the next moves become obvious.

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:26.780
Edison and his team started from the physical requirement.

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:32.720
Turn mechanical power from steam into steady 110V direct current at the lowest possible

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:33.020
loss.

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:39.100
The raw materials did not change, cast iron, copper, steel, the conventional design

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:43.960
multiplied the gap between those metals and the selling price through extra parts and extra steps.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:46.180
The first break was direct coupling.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:51.500
Instead of a belt or a rope, the dynamo shaft bolted straight to the engine crankshaft.

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:54.980
That single joint removed an entire transmission stage.

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:57.460
Belt slippage losses went with the belt.

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:00.620
The engine could run at the higher speed the dynamo needed,

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:03.360
because nothing in between had to be kept in tension.

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:07.040
Picture the old path as three machines pretending to be one.

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:10.000
Engine, then pulley, and belt, then generator.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:15.220
Each interface was a place to lose power, a place to buy hardware, and a place to send

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:15.760
a mechanic.

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:20.300
Direct coupling collapsed that chain to two frames sharing one shaft line.

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:25.540
The idiot index on the dynamo fell because a whole class of purchased parts and the

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:29.620
labor that installed them left the bill without any change in the iron or the copper.

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:37.400
The second move was the size itself. At 44,820 pounds and roughly 100 kilowatts, each

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:43.460
jumbo was 4 times larger than earlier machines. 44,000 pounds is a small locomotive's worth

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:49.240
of metal standing in one room, not a bench dynamo scaled up with a longer belt. The larger

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:54.500
armature and field allowed the same voltage with fewer turns of thicker copper. Fewer turns

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:55.280
cut resistance.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:59.680
Thicker copper, wound, and fewer passes also cut the labor of winding.

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:04.580
A further consequence of the larger diameter armature was that the magnetic field could

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:07.440
be produced with fewer ampere turns in the field coils.

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:12.440
That trimmed the amount of copper that had to be wound and insulated, which is a cut in

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:15.660
both material and hours, aimed at the same kilowatt.

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:20.980
The third move was to run identical units in parallel rather than build one giant machine.

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:25.700
parallel operation let any unit be taken offline for maintenance without

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:29.900
shutting the district. It also let the shop cast and machine identical frames

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:34.680
instead of a unique giant casting. Each engine dynamo pair could be started and

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:38.720
synchronized separately which avoided the single-point failure risk of one

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:43.360
enormous unit. The decision to cast identical frames in the same found rerun

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:47.840
reduced pattern making time. The same machining fixtures could be used on every

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:53.040
machine. Each of these choices attacked a different term in the cost stack, fewer parts,

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:59.400
less copper per kilowatt, and repeatable manufacturing. None of them changed the underlying iron copper

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:04.580
or steel. They changed how many times a shop had to touch that metal before a customer got light.

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:09.780
The direct coupling joint had to be aligned to tight tolerances so vibration would not destroy

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:15.160
the bearings. The team solved that by machining the engine and dynamo frames as matched pairs in

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:20.520
the same shop. Alignment stopped being a field improvisation and became a factory relationship

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:25.760
between two castings cut together. The larger armature demanded better insulation between

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:30.500
commutator segments. Micah Sheet supplied the dielectric strength at the temperatures

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:35.540
reached under load. Those engineering winds moved the realized costs closer to the material

00:06:35.540 --> 00:06:45.140
floor than any belt-driven predecessor had reached. Closer, not onto the floor. The

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:49.720
machining hours and the building around the machines all sat above that floor, and they

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:50.460
were supposed to.

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:54.880
What the redesign removed was the waste that analogy had treated as mandatory.

00:06:55.480 --> 00:07:01.060
Those three changes dropped the dynamo idiot index, but they left one expensive layer untouched.

00:07:02.046 --> 00:07:05.226
The station supplied its first paying customers on the opening day.

00:07:05.826 --> 00:07:08.626
It ran until a fire destroyed the building in 1890.

00:07:09.406 --> 00:07:15.186
One surviving jumbo, later displayed at the 1893 Chicago Fair, proved the direct couple

00:07:15.186 --> 00:07:18.266
design could operate for years with only routine attention.

00:07:18.786 --> 00:07:23.546
That machine also showed the layout tolerated the daily load swings of a commercial district

00:07:23.546 --> 00:07:27.386
without rapid bearing wear, once the initial alignment was achieved.

00:07:27.966 --> 00:07:32.626
The project bought a working template that other cities copied, yet the Copper Distribution

00:07:32.626 --> 00:07:37.226
network alone consumed a large share of the budget, because every service drop still

00:07:37.226 --> 00:07:39.626
had to be run through iron pipes under the street.

00:07:39.626 --> 00:07:45.006
The idiot index for the dynamo's themselves had come down from the earlier belt-driven pattern.

00:07:45.446 --> 00:07:51.006
The underground conduit system remained far above its own material floor. Copper and iron pipe

00:07:51.006 --> 00:07:56.566
have a commodity price. What the street actually paid for was hand excavation and pipe threading

00:07:56.566 --> 00:08:02.746
for every block. Those costs the dynamo redesign could not touch. Later stations would attack that

00:08:02.746 --> 00:08:08.526
remaining gap with different cable designs and different voltage choices. The 1882 installation

00:08:08.526 --> 00:08:12.426
and therefore demonstrated both the power and the boundary of the first redesign.

00:08:13.251 --> 00:08:17.591
You can drive a rotating machine toward its metal floor and still leave the network that

00:08:17.591 --> 00:08:19.551
feeds it priced like a custom trench.

00:08:20.131 --> 00:08:25.711
The same arithmetic, material floor versus joints and labor, now applies to any large rotating

00:08:25.711 --> 00:08:26.171
machine.

00:08:26.751 --> 00:08:31.211
A machine whose heaviest parts are iron and copper still carries most of its price in the

00:08:31.211 --> 00:08:33.131
joints and alignments between those parts.

00:08:33.831 --> 00:08:35.471
The metal is visible on a scale.

00:08:35.471 --> 00:08:41.611
the joints hide in drawings, a belt, a coupling, a flange, a hand-fit bearing seat, a coil

00:08:41.611 --> 00:08:46.811
wound because the last coil was wound that way. Direct coupling removed one entire class

00:08:46.811 --> 00:08:51.151
of joints and the labor that went with them. The engine and the dynamo stopped being

00:08:51.151 --> 00:08:56.671
neighbors connected by a wearable part and became one shaft line, machined as matched pairs

00:08:56.671 --> 00:09:02.011
so the alignment would survive real load. Parallel identical units turned a custom fabrication

00:09:02.011 --> 00:09:05.091
problem into a repeatable casting and machining run.

00:09:05.691 --> 00:09:07.031
Pattern making happened once.

00:09:07.731 --> 00:09:08.951
Fixtures stayed on the machine.

00:09:09.531 --> 00:09:14.031
A unit could come offline without darkening the district, which is an operating saving

00:09:14.031 --> 00:09:16.051
that never appears in a commodity quote.

00:09:16.671 --> 00:09:17.951
That is the portable split.

00:09:18.511 --> 00:09:21.451
Ask what the raw materials would cost if assembly were free.

00:09:21.851 --> 00:09:26.091
Then ask which interfaces are still on the drawing because a smaller machine once needed

00:09:26.091 --> 00:09:26.331
them.

00:09:26.891 --> 00:09:31.391
If the finished price sits many times above the metal, and you cannot point to a physical

00:09:31.391 --> 00:09:37.211
law that requires the extra parts, the index is pointing at process, not at all. Pearl

00:09:37.211 --> 00:09:41.711
Street did not get all the way to the wand number. It got far enough to show that the old

00:09:41.711 --> 00:09:46.731
transmission stage was a choice. The conduit work showed the next choice, sitting in the

00:09:46.731 --> 00:09:51.971
street, still priced like excavation rather than like copper. The same arithmetic now applies

00:09:51.971 --> 00:09:57.171
to any generator or motor whose bill of materials is dominated by structural metal and conductor.

00:09:57.171 --> 00:10:02.471
The next reduction will come from eliminating another interface, or from standardizing another

00:10:02.471 --> 00:10:07.851
sub-assembly, not from hoping the iron gets cheaper, which current large rotating machine

00:10:07.851 --> 00:10:12.031
still pays for an obsolete transmission stage that direct coupling could remove.

00:10:12.591 --> 00:10:14.531
That's first principles daily for today.

00:10:15.031 --> 00:10:19.651
If this changed how you see what things cost, share it with someone who depreciated and

00:10:19.651 --> 00:10:21.011
subscribe wherever you listen.

00:10:21.471 --> 00:10:22.671
I'm Patrick and Vancouver.

